      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to use the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon (which is what the figure describes) is always \\( 540^\\circ \\). We are given the measures of four of the angles: \\( 128^\\circ \\), \\( 92^\\circ \\), \\( 113^\\circ \\), and \\( 111^\\circ \\). Let's denote the measure of angle \\( P \\) as \\( x \\).\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a pentagon can be expressed as:\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the given angles:\n\\[\n128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ = 444^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute this sum into the equation:\n\\[\n444^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we subtract \\( 444^\\circ \\) from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nx = 540^\\circ - 444^\\circ = 96^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is \\(\\boxed{96}\\).",
