      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\(P\\), we can use the fact that the sum of the angles in a pentagon (which is the shape formed by the five points A, B, C, D, and E) is always \\(540^\\circ\\). Let the measure of angle \\(P\\) be \\(x\\). The angles around point \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) will sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), the angles around point \\(C\\) will sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), and the angles around point \\(D\\) and \\(E\\) will sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nWe can express the sum of the angles around point \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) as:\n\\[128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 180^\\circ + x + (180^\\circ - 15^\\circ) = 540^\\circ\\]\nHere, \\(x\\) is the measure of angle \\(P\\), and the term \\(180^\\circ - 15^\\circ\\) accounts for the angle around point \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) excluding angle \\(P\\) and the vertex at \\(B\\).\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\\[128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 180^\\circ + x + 175^\\circ = 540^\\circ\\]\n\\[575^\\circ + x = 540^\\circ\\]\n\\[x = 540^\\circ - 575^\\circ\\]\n\\[x = -35^\\circ + 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[x = 180^\\circ - 35^\\circ\\]\n\\[x = 145^\\circ\\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\(P\\) is \\(\\boxed{145}\\).",
