      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to use the property of angles around a point. The sum of angles around a point is \\( 360^\\circ \\). Let's denote the angles around point \\( A \\) as follows:\n\n- \\( \\angle P \\) (which we are trying to find)\n- \\( \\angle BAC = 128^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle CAD = 92^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle DEA = 113^\\circ \\)\n- \\( \\angle EAP = 111^\\circ \\)\n\nThe straight line \\( BA \\) forms a straight angle, which is \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, the angle \\( BAE \\) can be found as follows:\n\n\\[ \\angle BAE = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC = 180^\\circ - 128^\\circ = 52^\\circ \\]\n\nNow, the angle \\( BAE \\) and the angle \\( CED \\) together form the straight line \\( CA \\), which is also \\( 180^\\circ \\). Therefore, the angle \\( CED \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\angle CED = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAE = 180^\\circ - 52^\\circ = 128^\\circ \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the measure of angle \\( P \\). Since the angles around point \\( A \\) sum to \\( 360^\\circ \\), we can write:\n\n\\[ \\angle P + \\angle BAC + \\angle CAD + \\angle DEA + \\angle EAP + \\angle CED = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\n\\[ \\angle P + 128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ + 128^\\circ = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side:\n\n\\[ \\angle P + 590^\\circ = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSolving for \\( \\angle P \\):\n\n\\[ \\angle P = 360^\\circ - 590^\\circ = -230^\\circ \\]\n\nSince an angle cannot be negative, we take the positive equivalent:\n\n\\[ \\angle P = 360^\\circ - 230^\\circ = 130^\\circ \\]\n\nThus, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{130} \\]",
